In article <2b7802aawnr070@ark.abg.sub.org> ralf@ark.abg.sub.org (Ralf Stephan) writes:
   zerkle@toadflax.cs.ucdavis.edu (Dan Zerkle) writes:

   > Given the general nastiness about the game, I find it necessary to
   > turn invisible as soon as possible.

   But invisibility doesn't seem as useful as it was in 3.0.  Monsters
   move now in your general direction/room.

When playing NH3.1 I no longer try to get intrinsic invisibility because I
want to be able to enter shops! I find the cloak or the ring MUCH more
valuable as in 3.0. (I don't rob shops ... My usually played barbarian always
has a lawful touch. Makes it harder and more challenging ... O:-) )

   > In the future, I expect shopkeepers to welcome all customers,
   > invisible or not.  They can detect invisible.  They should just be
   > able to sell to anyone.

   Yes, seconded.  Why aren't invisible customers welcome, anyway?

I don't agree. It's just game balance: The advantage of being invisible
has to implicit a disadvantage! Getting invisible is quite simple. Why
would you then go after the ring or cloak after all? Of couse it's hard,
but I'm quite happy with it. And I especially like the new shop features.
Robbing shops was _far_ too easy! Now I even _buy_ (*gasp*) the things I
need. :-)

